n per task on its total attempts: q_t = (N_t p\u0304_t + \u00bd)/(N_t + 1) (equal n: (\u03a3f+\u00bd)/(\u03a3n+1)); never one \u00bd per model before averaging (that shrinks toward \u00bd by n/(n+1) and confines a one-attempt axis to a narrow band around the middle (no task can read easier than about one in four failing or harder than about three in four)); a count-based axis resolves positions only within about \u00b1log(2N_t+1), beyond which and under few attempts the joint fit places tasks; served waves keep their cut-time axis until the big fit's cut switches the recipe (ruled 4 Sep on experiment-design's measurement)","curve_of_record":"both reserved words are read off the average-rate curve: the expected failure rate over the tasks at a difficulty with the fitted task spread included (capability = its 50% crossing, reliability = its 1% crossing); the median-task crossing is the labelled second option, never under the bare word; fitting's crossings_of_record carries the average-rate reading as d1_of_record (export switched in the first week of September; d1_median_task_z beside it); a table that reads D99 from the typical (median-task) curve says so under a flag (ruled 4 Sep; direction of 3 Sep: the 1% crossing is the reliability readout, the Bayesian fit is preferred for averages, the median-task reading is an alternative)","curve_of_record_updated":"updated 9 Sep: the clause about fitting's pending export switch retired; axis_smoothing and every other key unchanged","qualified_use":"10 Sep: the bare reserved words name the failure curve's crossings only; a crossing of any other curve carries a qualifier at first use and never the bare word (e.g. 'alignment reliability' = the difficulty at which a misbehaviour rate crosses 1%, testbed-feasibility, 10 Sep); readouts of any crossing are the difficulty percent, never a count of tasks below or above (ruled 10 Sep)","enforced":{"golden set":"the 12 models of Definitions' golden set list (Qwen3 0.6B, 1.7B, 4B, 8B each plain and thinking; Claude Haiku 4.5 and Sonnet 5 each plain and thinking), derived from the registry field golden_set=true; forms: golden set, golden model(s), golden(-set) axis, golden fit(s), golden curve(s); on counts 'golden set (12)' counts models; every golden number carries the note 'a data point, not the difficulty definition' (ruled 3 Sep); not used: 'golden models' as a count noun, 'golden population', 'derisking set', 'golden frame' (ruled 3 Sep)","average-rate 1% crossing":"where the model's fitted average failure rate first reaches 1%","median-task 1% crossing":"where the median task's failure rate reaches 1%","average-rate 50% crossing":"where the fitted average failure rate reaches even odds","median-task 50% crossing":"where the median task reaches even odds","level pair (1%, 50%)":"two linked crossings of one trend, always written with concrete levels \u2014 never variables","cumulative 1% failure rate":"the hardest difficulty such that average failure over ALL easier tasks is still \u2264 1%","worst-stretch 1% failure rate":"the same, but EVERY trailing stretch of easier tasks must stay \u2264 1% \u2014 easy wins cannot bank budget for later failures","first task above 1% failure":"the last difficulty before the first single task failing more than 1%","cumulative X% failure rate":"the same machine at any other concrete anchor","99%-assured":"prefix: quoted at the value it holds with 99% posterior probability"},"soft":{"success-mass balance point":"everything the model solves, added up and read as one position on the difficulty axis \u2014 mean pass rate expressed as a difficulty","contested mass":"how much of the difficulty axis the model SOMETIMES solves and sometimes fails \u2014 the width of its struggle zone","easy-success subsidy":"cumulative minus worst-stretch: how much quoted range was earned by easy wins rather than held everywhere"},"formerly":{"reliable range":"cumulative 1% failure rate","debt-free range":"worst-stretch 1% failure rate","isotonic 1% crossing":"worst-stretch 1% failure rate","per-task mastered prefix":"first task above 1% failure","Bayesian \u2014 typical task":"median-task (curve label)","typical":"median","fitted 50% crossing":"average-rate 50% crossing","balance point":"success-mass balance point","D_eq":"success-mass balance point","Z / contested-zone mass":"contested mass","subsidy mass":"easy-success subsidy","assured90/99":"99%-assured"},"dropped_no_unification":["crossing gap","transition width","transition asymmetry","skew","the counting nouns for models and their settings","frontier reads (dark range, d2, dM, solved-mass edge, L)"],"scope":{"binds":"value labels \u2014 wherever a bare reserved word functions as the label of a displayed number (axis names, tooltip labels, table headers over numbers, stat-tile captions)","exempt_tool_names":"tool/site/page names denote subject matter, not measurements, and are exempt (task-pool in-flight ruling, scoped by difficulty, 28 Aug)","litmus":"if deleting the word would leave a number unlabeled, the reservation applies; if it would merely rename a surface, it does not","gloss_pattern":"a bare reserved word may gloss its own ruled metric when the full ruled name is adjacent (e.g. 'Capability: average-rate 50% crossing', average chain at ruled levels only \u2014 the CROSSINGS pattern, blessed)"},"crossings_plots_axis_names":{"ruled":"2026-09-18","D50":"the average-rate 50% crossing (the capability crossing): its name wherever the crossing is named by symbol (uppercase D, the ruling of 18 Sep 13:0x UK)","D99":"the average-rate 1% crossing (the reliability crossing), named by the success side: 99% of answers pass where 1% fail; the same quantity as the 1% crossing; its name wherever the crossing is named by symbol","scope":"the axes, keys and prose of the crossings plots (the review of the capability-vs-reliability page, 18 Sep 12:1x UK: the page name stays, the y-axis is called D50 and D99; 13:0x UK: uppercase D, and a scale is na